we're getting a rare look this morning  at evidence from one of the largest mass  kidnappings in US history three masked  gunmen seized a bus carrying 26 children  and their driver back in 1976 the  kidnappers drove them a hundred miles  from Chowchilla California to a rock  quarry they buried the children and the  driver alive 12 feet underground in a  moving truck while the driver and  children managed to dig themselves out  the 28 hour nightmare still haunts them  more than 40 years later our lead  national correspondent David beg note  takes an inside look at the case for 48  hours it was the summer of 1976 this bus  pulled into the small town of Chowchilla  California inside the bus were 26  traumatized children outside with a  frantic parents these children had been  through an unimaginable ordeal kidnapped  and buried alive in an old moving truck  that had been hidden beneath a rock  quarry six-year-old Larry Park was one  of those kids this man carried me off  the bus and he put me in my mom's arms  I felt I felt like I was finally safe  14-year old Michael Marshall and the bus  driver piled up mattresses that were in  the hole and after 16 hours in darkness  they managed to dig their way to safety  I'm giving it everything I got and all  the kids are cheering me on and you know  you can do it police immediately zeroed  in on 24 year old Fred woods he was the  son of the man who owned the rock quarry  and two of Fred woods his friends James  and Richard Schoenfeld Jill clingy is an  assistant district attorney in Alameda  County fred woods had keys to that  quarry investigators executed a warrant  to search fred Woods's father's estate  and they found a treasure trove of  evidence they're now shared with 48  hours  this crime was planned out for a year  and a half in intricate detail you  actually have a document  labeled plan and it sets out the way  they were going to commit the kidnapping  they also recovered a draft of a ransom  note they were going to ask for five  million dollars from the state of  California but the kidnappers were never  able to deliver their demand when they  tried to call the Chowchilla Police  Department because of the number of  calls that were coming in worldwide the  phone lines were jammed they couldn't  get through so they took a nap and by  the time they woke up they saw in the  news that the kids had been found so  they were never able to request their  ransom with the overwhelming evidence  against them the three men were arrested  all came from well-off families who  lived in some of San Francisco's nicest  suburbs they're young they're white  they're wealthy I think it added a  component of fascination to the story  because it was so unlikely that three  men such as these would commit such a  treacherous crime and David is at the  table with us this morning good morning  David what do we know about the motive  of the kidnappers exactly so this is  really interesting and this is a quote  here they said we needed multiple  victims to get multiple millions and so  we picked children because they're  precious they did put a lot of thought  into it you know back then people didn't  really talk as openly about therapy did  they offer therapy to these kids you  know Gil they took them to Disneyland  that's what they did it's because the  thought was if we go to Disneyland and  take a picture and you see Mickey Mouse  and everything's great you might forget  about what just happened and Fred Woods  was denied parole when you hear earlier  he was going to parole but he was denied  for a 17th time you know one of the  reasons why he was running a Christmas  tree farm from prison now wait a minute  that's not illegal  you just have to ask the warden for  permission he didn't another two  kidnappers they're free they're out they  followed the rules got parole all right  David thank you you can see David's 48  hours report live to tell the Chow  chilla kidnapping Saturday night at  10:00 9:00 c